{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792854970,"nonce":"ASs4mPl2xxx8yVtMbaoJHNAYphE=","title":"Python: bm_ai runs dynamic because generators yield boxes and runtime-length tuples are boxed","message":"bm_ai (CI 3.05x behind PyPy on Linux; NUC 39.9 against PyPy 18.2 ms) builds every permutation as `tuple(pool[i] for i in indices[:r])` inside a generator and checks it with two sets of `vec[i] + i`. A generator is Ty::Gen, a fiber yielding dynamic values, and `tuple(...)` of anything without a settled shape is Ty::Object (zb_box_tuple of a list of boxes), so `vec` is dynamic: the profile of the program thread is zb_any_getitem_i64, zb_arith_i64, zb_any_eq, zb_number_i64 and zb_box_i64, the sets are dynamic (zb_set_add/find/insert on boxes), and allocation is about a third of it.\n\nThe same kernel written with typed lists and no generators runs 20 to 22 ms against PyPy 12 ms on that code, so typing alone is worth about 2x and the allocation volume is the rest (1f4e08c).\n\nWhat it needs: a homogeneous tuple of runtime length (a list header with tuple identity, held as itself by locals, parameters, results and fields and boxed for every other reader, the way Ty::MaybeList is), and a generator type that carries what it yields (a fiber step carries a 62-bit payload, so pointer-held yields can travel raw; ints and floats stay boxed).","files":null}]}